// Pages (v4) for section 3 (q3_1..q3_6) and section 4 (q4_1..q4_5).
// Assigned onto the PAGES object declared in pages-index.js. Facts grounded in
// depth-p2.js, qbank-p2.js, 04-guide.js and the repo docs (ALCANCE/FEATURES/
// SCHEMA/ARCHITECTURE/TASKS/REFERENCES). Do not edit the depth/qbank/guide files.

PAGES.q3_1 = {
  intro: "This page decides how users prove who they are: server-side sessions or stateless JWT tokens. The choice sets how much auth code you review, how logout and account blocking behave, and how the AIDLC workflow rates the auth unit. It explains both mechanisms, the 2025-2026 consensus, and what a 1-2 user email/password demo actually needs.",
  sections: [
    { h: "How sessions work", b: "With sessions, the server stores the session (in the database or in memory) and the browser holds only a cookie with the session id. On every request the browser sends the cookie, the server looks up the session, and the server knows who the caller is. The session is server-side state: the client holds no secret, just a reference.\n\nThe big operational advantage is revocation. Logout, or banning an account, means deleting the session server-side, and the user is out instantly — no waiting for a token to expire, no blacklist to maintain. Both candidate frameworks support this natively: Django has built-in session auth, and FastAPI gets it through starlette SessionMiddleware or small libraries. CSRF protection for cookie-based auth is a known, solved pattern.\n\nThe cost is that the server holds state, and the cookie needs sane flags (secure, httpOnly, sameSite). For one backend serving one domain this is the boring, default path — exactly what a demo wants." },
    { h: "How JWT works", b: "JWT is a signed, self-contained token: header, payload, signature. The server verifies the signature and trusts the claims inside — no session lookup required. That makes it stateless, which is genuinely useful for mobile apps and for distributed backends that cannot share a session store.\n\nThe costs are real. Revocation: a logged-out or banned user keeps a valid token until it expires, unless you build a blocklist — server-side state that quietly defeats the statelessness. Refresh-token rotation adds more code to review and more failure modes. Secret management: the signing key is a live credential that must be protected and rotated.\n\nThe effort and risk columns in the option table reflect this: sessions are roughly half a day with low risk; JWT is 1-2 days with medium risk, mostly from revocation and refresh logic." },
    { h: "The 2025-2026 consensus", b: "The current industry consensus (Stytch, Authgear, and the wider community) is consistent: for a single-domain web application whose server you control, sessions are simpler and safer. JWT shines when you have multiple clients or domains, or a mobile-first architecture where a cookie jar is awkward.\n\nYour project is the textbook sessions case: one backend, one web or Telegram client, email/password login, one or two users. ALCANCE.md says the auth mechanism is defined when the stack is decided — so this answer is a preference statement that Step 8 (TASKS.md) and the AIDLC gate will consume. Choosing sessions does not close any door; both Django and FastAPI make it the low-effort default." },
    { h: "Common pitfalls", b: "Pitfall one: choosing JWT 'because it is modern' without a mobile client or a multi-domain need — you pay the revocation and refresh costs for no benefit. Pitfall two: forgetting revocation in the demo — a 'banned' user who still has access is a bad look for the professor. Pitfall three: letting the AI agent add refresh-token rotation, email verification, or password-reset flows you never asked for; scope the AI explicitly.\n\nPitfall four: mixing both mechanisms (sessions for web plus JWT for an API) — a legitimate production pattern, but it doubles the auth code you must review. For this demo, pick one. Pitfall five: treating auth as the security story — it is only the identity layer." },
    { h: "What a good answer includes", b: "State the choice explicitly: sessions. Add one line on why: instant revocation, framework-native support, and less code to review. Optionally note that CSRF is handled by the framework, and say explicitly whether password reset is in scope or you ship a pre-seeded demo user — saying 'demo with a pre-seeded user' is legitimate and stops the AI from adding reset logic.\n\nThe deeper point the professor will test: auth only identifies the user. The thing that actually protects data is the data-scoping rule — every query filtered by user_id, the server never returns another user's rows. State that the rule stands regardless of the mechanism. That closes the Step 8 auth item and pre-fills the AIDLC requirement questions." }
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Do you foresee multiple clients (web + bot + app) hitting the same API?", info: "Multiple clients nudges toward JWT; one client makes sessions trivially correct." },
    { q: "Do you want to implement logout/ban instantly without token blacklists?", info: "Sessions give this for free." },
    { q: "Password reset: in scope or 'demo with a pre-seeded user'?", info: "Skipping reset logic is legitimate for a demo — say it explicitly so the AI does not add it." }
  ],
  links: [
    "JWT vs sessions — Stytch — https://stytch.com/blog/jwts-vs-sessions-which-is-right-for-you/",
    "JWT vs session auth in Django 2025 — https://medium.com/@anas-issath/jwt-vs-session-authentication-in-django-which-one-should-you-use-in-2025-7e0fd4ea195f",
    "docs/ALCANCE.md — Roles & Permissions (repo)"
  ]
};

PAGES.q3_2 = {
  intro: "This page decides where receipt photos live: a folder on the same machine as the app (local disk) or an object-storage service like S3. The answer determines setup effort, where the files are when the professor runs the demo, and how backup works. It covers both options, the demo recommendation, and the interface rule that keeps S3 a later swap.",
  sections: [
    { h: "How local disk works", b: "Receipt images live in a folder next to the app (for example ./data/receipts). The path is a configuration value — an env var such as RECEIPT_DIR — not a hardcoded string. Backup is simply copying the folder, and copying the project copies the receipts along with it.\n\nThe database stores only the path, per SCHEMA.md: receipt.image is TEXT (a path or URL), never the binary. Keeping the blob out of the DB keeps the database light and the schema simple. There are zero services, zero credentials, and zero SDK calls — the file system is the storage API." },
    { h: "How object storage works", b: "Object storage (S3-compatible) means a service — AWS S3, self-hosted MinIO, Backblaze B2 — plus credentials, SDK calls, and signed URLs for private reads. It is production-grade and effectively unlimited in size, but it is also a second system to run and configure.\n\nThe real value appears only when multiple servers share the same files, or when you need CDN-scale delivery. For a single machine demo, it is a service and credentials with no beneficiary. Effort reflects this: minutes for local disk versus 1-2 days for object storage, with medium risk." },
    { h: "The demo answer", b: "For one machine, local disk is the standard answer, and it matches the docs: ARCHITECTURE.md draws File Storage as 'local disk (demo)' inside the Data Layer. When the professor runs the demo, the images are exactly where the app expects them, because they travel with the project.\n\nThe one engineering rule that matters: keep the storage layer behind a small interface — a save function and a read function. The rest of the app never knows where files live. S3 then becomes a later swap behind the same interface, one env var away, not a rewrite." },
    { h: "What the professor's machine changes", b: "The grading machine runs your code, not your laptop. With local disk, the receipts folder must be part of the deliverable: if the professor copies only the database, every image path breaks. Decide now that the project folder — code plus data directory — is the unit you hand over.\n\nAlso decide whether to ship sample receipts. A few pre-loaded demo photos make the demo reproducible without hunting for paper receipts in front of the professor, and they exercise the vision extraction path on demand. Document the RECEIPT_DIR value in STACK.md later so the professor can point the app at a folder of their own. Use relative paths from the project root (for example ./data/receipts, not /home/user/...) so the folder works on any machine without reconfiguration. Sample receipts also let you test the delete-on-discard path (q4_4) safely before the demo." },
    { h: "Common pitfalls", b: "Pitfall one: deleting a receipt row but leaving the image file on disk — the discard path (see q4_4) must remove the file too, or you leak receipt images over time. Pitfall two: hardcoding the folder path instead of reading RECEIPT_DIR-style config, which breaks when the project moves to the professor's machine.\n\nPitfall three: serving files directly from the web server instead of through the API — going through the API keeps access control simple (only the owner, only when logged in). Pitfall four: choosing S3 'for the resume' and spending demo days on credentials and SDK code that add nothing the professor can see." },
    { h: "What a good answer includes", b: "Confirm local disk, with the path configurable via env var. Decide the folder structure — user_id/year-month/ is the readable pattern for a demo, since it groups files by owner and month. Decide who can access the files: the API only, or also the professor via file manager — serving through the API is the simpler access-control story.\n\nFinally, note the connection to q4_4: the discard path deletes the file, not just the DB row. This answer confirms the File Storage container in ARCHITECTURE.md and closes the Step 8 storage item." }
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Will more than one machine ever need to read the same receipt?", info: "If yes (e.g., professor machine + yours), decide which one is the source of truth." },
    { q: "What folder structure: per-user folders or flat with hashed names?", info: "user_id/year-month/ is the readable pattern for a demo." },
    { q: "Who can access the files — the API only, or also the professor via file manager?", info: "Serving files through the API keeps access control simple." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md — File Storage container (repo)",
    "docs/SCHEMA.md — Receipt.image stores a path, not binary (repo)"
  ]
};

PAGES.q3_3 = {
  intro: "This page decides the default display currency (COP or USD) and where conversion rates come from. Multi-currency is a Phase 3 feature, but the schema already stores the original currency, so the decision shapes the schema and the docs now. It covers the store-original/convert-on-display rule, three rate sources, and a known contradiction in ALCANCE.md that your answer must resolve.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The design rule", b: "Both ALCANCE.md and SCHEMA.md state the same rule: store the original amount and its currency, and convert only when displaying. The original amount is always the truth and is never overwritten. The Receipt stores the purchase currency (one purchase = one currency), and the User has a preferred display currency; conversion happens on the fly, for display only.\n\nThis rule is what makes the whole question low-stakes: you cannot get the data model wrong later. Whatever rate source you pick today only affects what number appears on screen — the stored data stays correct forever." },
    { h: "Source 1: live API rates", b: "A live API such as Frankfurter (free, no key; 200+ currencies from ~84 central banks, including COP) or exchangerate.host returns current rates on every request. Accuracy is perfect and maintenance is zero — until the demo needs internet.\n\nThe costs: internet is required on every display, an outage breaks the dashboard, and you are calling a third party during the professor's demo. Effort is about one day, with medium risk. It is the right choice only if the demo will always run online." },
    { h: "Sources 2 and 3: static rates and manual entry", b: "Static rates are a small JSON file bundled in the repo (for example usd_cop: 4100, updated by hand). They always work, including fully offline, cost nothing per call, and take minutes to set up. The trade-off: they are stale by definition — the rate drifts until someone edits the file.\n\nManual entry is the same idea with a settings field instead of a repo edit: the user types the rate when it changes. Simpler still, with the same staleness risk plus a human who forgets. Both are Low complexity, Low risk — the demo-safe pair." },
    { h: "How the UI shows currency", b: "Expense lines should show what the user needs without clutter: the original amount with its currency, and the converted value in the display currency when they differ — for example '$4.50 (18.675 COP)'. The settings page holds the default display currency selector and, for static or manual sources, the rate itself with its update date.\n\nIncome follows the same rule as expenses — SCHEMA.md stores the original currency and converts on display — so the balance analysis ('earned 2M COP, spent 1.2M — balance 800k', from ALCANCE.md) stays consistent even when incomes and expenses arrive in different currencies. Keep the conversion logic in one module; switching rate sources later then touches exactly one file. For display, round converted values to two decimals in the display currency and always keep the original amount visible on the line, so the user can verify the math. Dashboard totals are computed at query time in the display currency — nothing is ever stored converted." },
    { h: "The contradiction to resolve", b: "ALCANCE.md currently says the AI 'handles conversion with current rates' — which implies a live source. If you pick static or manual, that line must be updated later or your docs contradict your decision, and the professor may catch it. Your answer should name the choice AND the doc fix.\n\nThe realistic demo path: static or manual now, with Frankfurter behind a toggle when online. The schema supports switching later without a migration, because the original amount and currency are already stored." },
    { h: "What a good answer includes", b: "Name the default display currency — COP is the natural default for this project, with USD as the second currency. Name the source: static or manual for the demo, Frankfurter when online. Name the two or three currencies that actually appear in your expenses — COP plus USD keeps the feature real without the multi-currency rabbit hole.\n\nDescribe where the rate display lives: a settings page line such as '1 USD = 4,150 COP (static, updated 2026-08)' is honest and demo-safe. And write the ALCANCE.md fix: replace the 'current rates' phrasing with your chosen source." }
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Will the demo run with internet or offline?", info: "Offline demo → static/manual; online demo → Frankfurter is free." },
    { q: "Which currencies actually appear in your expenses — COP only, or USD too?", info: "Two currencies keep the feature real without the multi-currency rabbit hole." },
    { q: "Where does the conversion rate display live (settings page)?", info: "Showing '1 USD = 4,150 COP (static, updated 2026-08)' is honest and demo-safe." }
  ],
  links: [
    "Frankfurter — free exchange rates API — https://frankfurter.dev/",
    "docs/ALCANCE.md — Currencies (repo)"
  ]
};

PAGES.q3_4 = {
  intro: "This page confirms how spending limits are created: a settings UI in the MVP, and chat-created limits later through the agent (Phase 3). Both features are Phase 3 in FEATURES.md, and TASKS.md Step 4 has open checkboxes waiting for this confirmation. It covers the UI-first and chat-first paths, the shared data model, and the limit behavior your docs already define.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The two features", b: "Feature 10 (spending limits) and feature 16 (agent-created limits via chat) are both Phase 3 in FEATURES.md. Feature 10 says limits are 'created via settings UI (MVP); the agent can also create/edit limits via chat (Phase 3)'. Feature 16 is the chat path: the user says 'keep eating out under $50/month' and the agent creates or edits the limit row.\n\nSo the question is not whether limits exist — it is the order and the mechanism. The proposal on the table: UI first in the MVP settings screen, chat later. TASKS.md Step 4 has an open checkbox (it appears twice, duplicated) that this confirmation closes." },
    { h: "UI-first (recommended)", b: "A settings screen with a monthly limit per category: a name, an amount, a period, and simple validation (limit > 0). Progress is displayed as 'eating out: $38 of $50'. The flow is deterministic and reviewable — it cannot fail because an AI tool call failed.\n\nThis is the recommendation: UI in the MVP, chat as a Phase 3 extension. The demo's core story — receipts, audio, chat, graphs — must not depend on the agent's tool-calling reliability. Prove the basics first, then add the impressive part." },
    { h: "Chat-first (the alternative)", b: "Chat-first means the agent creates and edits limit rows through a tool call when the user expresses a preference in conversation. It makes a better demo moment, but it couples the feature to agent reliability: if tool-calling fails mid-demo, the feature fails in front of the professor.\n\nIt also adds prompt-design work (parsing 'under $50', 'per month', 'eating out' into a structured limit) before the deterministic path exists. Keep it as the Phase 3 showcase, not the MVP foundation." },
    { h: "The shared data model", b: "Whichever path creates limits, both write to the SAME SpendingLimit table — the data model must be identical so the later feature is additive, not a redesign. SCHEMA.md locks the shape: amount in the user's preferred currency, period 'weekly' or 'monthly', and the XOR rule — exactly one of category_id or subcategory_id must be set, never both, never neither, enforced by the UI and a database constraint.\n\nOne dimension is enough for the MVP: per category, per month. The schema already tolerates weekly, but adding more dimensions now multiplies UI and validation work without demo value." },
    { h: "What the agent needs to know", b: "When feature 16 arrives, the agent parses natural language ('keep eating out under $50') into amount, period, and target, then writes through a tool call to the same table. The XOR rule must be enforced in the tool-call validation too — the agent cannot create a limit with both a category and a subcategory.\n\nLimits are per user (user_id FK, per SCHEMA.md indexes), so the agent only ever touches the caller's rows. Progress reporting is part of the feature: chat answers like 'you have $12 left for eating out this month' make the awareness design visible. Plan the demo script now: create a limit in settings, register a receipt that passes it, and watch the report appear." },
    { h: "Behavior and visibility", b: "What happens when spending passes the limit? ALCANCE.md answers: 'AI-assisted awareness, not rigid hard limits' — the AI tracks and reports progress; it does not block purchases. State that explicitly so the AI agent does not invent hard blocking.\n\nWhere does the user see progress? A small progress bar on the dashboard makes the feature visible without a new screen. Your answer should confirm: UI first, chat later, report-don't-block, dashboard visibility, and note the Step 4 checkbox (duplicated line) gets cleaned up when you close it. Limits are database rows in the SpendingLimit table, so they survive restarts and are the same data the chat agent will read and edit in Phase 3. The AI's reports appear both on the dashboard and in chat answers, matching feature 10's 'AI tracks and reports progress'." }
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "What happens when spending passes the limit — block, warn, or just report?", info: "ALCANCE.md says 'AI-assisted awareness, not rigid hard limits' — reporting is the design." },
    { q: "Limit granularity: per category, per month — anything else (per week)?", info: "Keep it to one dimension for the MVP." },
    { q: "Where does the user SEE progress — dashboard card or settings?", info: "A small progress bar on the dashboard makes the feature visible without a new screen." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/FEATURES.md — Phase 3 (10, 16) (repo)",
    "docs/SCHEMA.md — SpendingLimit entity, XOR rule (repo)"
  ]
};

PAGES.q3_5 = {
  intro: "This page decides where feature 14 gets its prices: answering 'how much does rice cost?' needs a data source. ARCHITECTURE.md still lists 'Price source — to define' in its pending decisions, and this question closes that TBD. It covers the four options, the DANE reference, and the demo-safe recommendation.",
  sections: [
    { h: "What feature 14 needs", b: "Feature 14 is the Phase 3 supermarket price reference: the agent answers questions like 'how much does rice cost?' by consulting a data source of product prices. FEATURES.md leaves the source as 'to define' and explicitly says 'live online scraping not recommended'.\n\nA price source is a small lookup table: item name → reference price → date (and ideally a source note). The demo value is in the chat answer — the agent shows it knows what rice costs — not in real-time market data. The source choice is also the answer to the professor's 'where do the prices come from?', so it is part of the project's credibility, not just a technical detail." },
    { h: "Option A: static CSV dataset (recommended)", b: "A hand-made CSV with roughly 50 common Colombian items — rice, eggs, milk, bread, chicken, coffee — each with a reference price and a date. It is bundled in the repo, works fully offline, is predictable in the demo, and takes about half a day to build.\n\nYou own the data quality, which is the point: base the item list on what YOU actually buy, so the demo is personally relatable. Prices can be anchored to a real citation (see the DANE section) without pretending they are live." },
    { h: "Options B, C, D", b: "Option B is manual entries — the user types prices as they shop. Trivial to build, but the dataset stays tiny and sparse. Option C is live scraping — real prices, but fragile selectors, terms-of-service risk, days of effort, and High risk; FEATURES.md explicitly says not recommended, so choosing it means overriding your own docs.\n\nOption D is skipping the feature — honest if the time is better spent on the core demo. The cost is losing a Phase 3 selling point. All three are defensible; only C is genuinely dangerous." },
    { h: "The DANE reference", b: "Colombia's official statistics office, DANE, publishes canasta familiar statistics — the national food-price index. It is authoritative and citable, but it is index-level data, not per-supermarket-item prices. You cannot query 'rice in Bogota today' from it.\n\nWhat it IS good for: a citation. A CSV line documented as '2026-08, DANE canasta categories + local market' is a defensible answer when the professor asks where the prices come from. The docs already use this framing — the guide glossary lists 'DANE canasta' as the reference for the price-source feature." },
    { h: "Where the CSV lives and how it loads", b: "Bundle the file in the repo (for example data/prices.csv) and load it at startup into memory — fifty rows are nothing. Normalize item keys (lowercase, singular) so 'rice', 'Rice', and 'arroz' all match the same row; add the Spanish names as aliases since expenses arrive in Spanish.\n\nWhen an item is unknown, the agent answers honestly — 'I don't have a price for that yet' — instead of inventing one; an invented price would corrupt the reference feature's credibility. Each row carries price, currency (COP default), date, and source, and every answer should cite the dataset date so the professor sees the data is dated, not live. Loading happens once at startup into an in-memory map keyed by normalized item name; if the file is missing, the feature degrades gracefully — the agent simply answers 'price reference unavailable' instead of crashing. Define the agent tool call now: get_price(item) returns price, currency, date, and source, which is the same shape as a CSV row." },
    { h: "What a good answer includes", b: "Pick the option explicitly — the recommendation is A (static CSV) with a DANE citation in the docs. Sketch the 50-item list (based on your own shopping). Decide what date and source each price carries, for example '2026-08, DANE canasta categories + local market'.\n\nDecide where the price appears: the chat answer is the Phase 3 showcase; keep it out of the expense line for the MVP. This answer resolves the last TBD in ARCHITECTURE.md and pre-fills the Step 8 price-source item." }
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Which 50 items would your CSV contain?", info: "Base it on what YOU buy — it makes the demo personally relatable." },
    { q: "What date/source does each price carry?", info: "'2026-08, DANE canasta categories + local market' is a defensible citation." },
    { q: "Where does the price appear — chat answer, expense line, both?", info: "Chat answer is the Phase 3 showcase; keep it out of the expense line for MVP." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md — Pending decisions (repo)",
    "docs/FEATURES.md — Phase 3 (14) (repo)",
    "DANE — Precios y costos — https://www.dane.gov.co/index.php/estadisticas-por-tema/precios-y-costos"
  ]
};

PAGES.q3_6 = {
  intro: "This page locks the out-of-scope list so the AI agent never 'helpfully' adds features you did not plan. Scope is a defense: every feature you add is code to review, a demo path that can break, and a document section to defend. It covers the four locked exclusions, the Phase 3 candidates habit, and how the OpenCode prompt treats your list.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The locked list", b: "ALCANCE.md excludes four things. Bank API integration — auto-import from the bank — means PSD2/OAuth flows, credentials, sandbox accounts, and redirects: a project of its own. Investments and debt tracking is a whole new domain with its own rules and data model. Bill splitting adds multi-user complexity — who owes whom, partial payments, settlement. Recurring auto-detection is ML-ish pattern detection over spending history.\n\nNuance worth knowing: FEATURES.md Phase 3 feature 11 (recurring expenses) exists, activated manually by the user or by an AI suggestion that asks 'want to make this recurring?'. What ALCANCE.md excludes is the auto-detection part; a manual recurring flag remains a Phase 3 candidate if you want it. If you keep that nuance, add a one-line footnote in ALCANCE.md distinguishing 'recurring expenses (Phase 3 feature 11)' from 'recurring auto-detection (out of scope)' so the docs do not contradict each other." },
    { h: "The cost of each temptation", b: "Run the numbers before adding anything. Bank API integration: OAuth/PSD2 flows, sandbox accounts, credential storage, redirect handling — weeks of work and a demo path that depends on a third-party bank sandbox. Investments and debt: new tables, valuation rules, tax concepts — a new domain that doubles the schema review.\n\nBill splitting: multi-user transactions, partial payments, settlement states — changes to the schema and every query that touches ownership. Recurring auto-detection: pattern detection over time series needs history data you do not have yet, and it is exactly the kind of ML-ish feature that fails unpredictably in demos. Every one of these is a demo path that can break in front of the professor." },
    { h: "Why scope is a defense", b: "Every feature added later is: code you must review line by line (REFERENCES.md's review checklist applies to every unit), a demo path that can break at the worst moment, and a document section you must defend. The professor grades a demo that works; a broken extra feature hurts more than a missing one.\n\nThe pre-semester plan (TASKS.md) is explicit: scope defined now, zero code before the professor decides the platform. The out-of-scope list is the same discipline applied to the AI agent." },
    { h: "Phase 3 candidates, not scope creep", b: "Tempting ideas belong in a 'Phase 3 candidates' note, not in the MVP. Write each one down as 'idea, not MVP' — a monthly summary email, per-category budgets, savings goals, Google Keep integration (already feature 15). The professor sees ambition without scope creep, and the list is ready when the core demo is stable.\n\nThe qbank example is the model: 'Confirmed. Temptation: monthly summary email — idea for Phase 3, not MVP.' One line per temptation, no debate." },
    { h: "The AI guardrail", b: "The AI agent will suggest features during construction — that is what agents do. Your explicit list is the guardrail it must respect: the OpenCode prompt treats it as a hard constraint, so confirming the list here is what actually stops scope creep later. Every item on the locked list is a feature the agent must NOT implement, even if asked.\n\nIf the agent proposes something new anyway, the answer is the same one you prepare for the professor: 'we decided X because Y; it is a Phase 3 candidate.' When you write the OpenCode prompt, phrase the constraint as a hard list — 'these features are out of scope and must never be implemented or suggested: ...' — the clearer the list, the less the agent improvises." },
    { h: "What a good answer includes", b: "Confirm the four ALCANCE.md exclusions by name. Add your own temptations, each marked MVP or Phase 3. Predict which out-of-scope item the professor will ask about — bank integration is the usual suspect — and write the one-line answer now.\n\nKeep the answer short: this question is about restraint. A tight list that the AI can treat as a constraint is worth more than a long essay." }
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "List anything you are tempted to add — then mark each as MVP or Phase 3.", info: "Writing the temptation down removes the risk of mid-project scope creep." },
    { q: "Which out-of-scope item would the professor most likely ask about?", info: "Prepare a one-line answer: 'we decided X because Y; it is a Phase 3 candidate'." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/ALCANCE.md — Out of Scope (repo)",
    "docs/FEATURES.md — Phase 3 features (repo)"
  ]
};

PAGES.q4_1 = {
  intro: "This is the AIDLC workflow's first question, and it decides auth, data scoping, and the entire design depth. Your docs currently contradict each other: ALCANCE.md still documents a full Admin role, while the Step 5 decision explicitly excludes admin. This page explains the contradiction, the two ways to resolve it, and the data-scoping rule that applies either way.",
  sections: [
    { h: "Why this question comes first", b: "'Just me' versus 'multi-user login' changes everything downstream: whether you need an auth system at all, how every query is scoped, how many personas the demo must show, and how much ceremony the AIDLC workflow applies. The workflow stops at this gate and waits for your answer.\n\nAnswering here pre-fills the workflow's requirement-verification-questions file, so the gate opens instantly when you run the OpenCode prompt after the trip. This is the highest-leverage answer in the whole questionnaire. If you leave it unanswered, the workflow asks it again mid-semester — when you are busiest — and every diagram and story that depends on actors waits on it." },
    { h: "The contradiction in your docs", b: "ALCANCE.md, Roles & Permissions, still documents a full Admin role: separate from User accounts, with a system stats dashboard (total registered users, total expenses aggregated, expenses grouped by category across all users, expenses over time, AI API usage), user management (list, create, disable), and configuration of the shared AI API key.\n\ndocs/step5-plan.md, Q1, says the opposite: only the regular user is an actor; 'Admin exists in the product but is deliberately excluded from the architecture diagrams (project decision)'. Both documents are in the same repo — the professor can read both. Your answer must pick a side and schedule the doc fix." },
    { h: "How to resolve it", b: "Option 1: confirm the exclusion and later update ALCANCE.md — the Roles & Permissions section, and any Phase 0 feature mentioning user management. Option 2: keep a minimal admin view in scope after all, which also means adding admin to the architecture diagrams.\n\nThe recommendation is option 1. The demo is single-user focused; an admin dashboard adds a second persona, extra screens, and demo time spent on statistics instead of the AI features that make the project interesting. The shared AI API key configuration (feature 0b) can live in a simple env var or settings file instead of an admin screen." },
    { h: "What happens at the gate", b: "The workflow's first question feeds more than the requirements file: it decides the actors section of your diagrams, the user stories, and the depth of the auth unit (see q4_2). Answer it once here and every later stage consumes it — that is the point of pre-answering.\n\nOne schema detail to settle: SCHEMA.md's Role entity still lists 'admin' as a role value. With admin excluded, decide whether the Role table keeps two rows (harmless, future-proof if the professor changes their mind) or collapses to one. The recommendation: keep the table as designed — the data model costs nothing and stays compatible with both decisions. Mockups (TASKS.md Step 7) also simplify: with one persona you draw only the user screens — upload, review, dashboard, chat, settings — and skip any admin screens entirely." },
    { h: "The data-scoping rule stands regardless", b: "Whichever way you resolve the roles question, the data-scoping rule from ALCANCE.md applies: every query is filtered by user_id, and the server never returns another user's rows. This is the line that actually protects users — auth only identifies them (see q3_1).\n\nEven the admin dashboard, if it existed, would show only aggregated statistics, never individual expense data. State the rule in your answer; it is the security story the professor will check." },
    { h: "What a good answer includes", b: "Confirm the choice explicitly: multi-user email/password login with every query filtered by user_id; admin excluded, per step5-plan Q1. Then note the fix: ALCANCE.md's Roles & Permissions section (and any Phase 0 user-management wording) must be updated later so the docs agree — add that to TASKS.md Step 8's doc sweep.\n\nIf you are unsure whether the professor could require an admin view for the grade, write it in the proposal as an open question for the professor instead of guessing. Either way, the answer is: one persona, one demo story." }
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Who is the demo persona — a single user, or also an admin operator?", info: "One persona = one demo story. Two personas = double the screens." },
    { q: "If admin is excluded, which ALCANCE.md lines must be updated later?", info: "Roles & Permissions section and any Phase 0 feature mentioning user management." },
    { q: "Could the professor REQUIRE an admin view for the grade?", info: "If uncertain, note it in the proposal as an open question for the professor." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/ALCANCE.md — Roles & Permissions (repo)",
    "docs/step5-plan.md — Q1 decision (repo)"
  ]
};

PAGES.q4_2 = {
  intro: "This page pre-answers the AIDLC question about complexity ratings — Minimal, Standard, or Comprehensive — for each core unit of the project. Honest ratings control how many documents and approval gates the workflow generates, so this answer shapes your entire semester. It covers what each rating means and a suggested rating per unit.",
  sections: [
    { h: "How AIDLC adapts its depth", b: "The workflow (AGENTS.md, Requirements Analysis) scales its ceremony to your ratings. Minimal is for simple, clear requests — roughly one page of requirements. Standard is the normal case — functional and non-functional requirements with the usual documents. Comprehensive is for complex, high-risk work — traceability, scenarios, and quality attributes, with more approval gates.\n\nThe rating you give each unit controls how many documents the workflow produces and how many times it stops for your review. It is a dial, not a grade: the same unit rated Comprehensive generates far more paperwork than rated Standard. For example, photo extraction as Standard produces a requirements page; as Comprehensive it adds traceability and scenario documents plus extra approval gates." },
    { h: "The suggested ratings", b: "Auth/login = M: multi-user with sessions, but standard, well-trodden patterns. Photo extraction = C: external vision API, uploads, error cases, retries, prompt contracts — the riskiest unit. Audio/text parse = M. Auto-categorization = M: prompt design plus a user override path. Income registration = S: pure CRUD, one table, no AI.\n\nChat + graphs = C: tool-calling, chart generation, no-data cases, long answers — the second risky unit. Dashboard = M: queries plus Chart.js rendering. This mirrors the qbank example: auth M, photo C, audio/text M, categories M, income S, chat+graphs C, dashboard M. The dashboard also shows the charts users see first, so M is fair: the queries are standard and the rendering is a library call." },
    { h: "What each rating produces", b: "Minimal produces an intent analysis — a short statement of what the unit does — and one review point. Standard produces functional and non-functional requirement documents with a normal review. Comprehensive adds a traceability matrix, scenario descriptions, and quality attributes, and it stops the workflow at extra gates for your approval.\n\nConcretely: photo extraction rated Comprehensive forces the error-case scenarios you already decided in q4_4 (blurry photo, API failure, retry, fallback). Chat+graphs rated Comprehensive forces the no-data cases and tool-call failure paths. Income rated S produces a simple CRUD spec and nothing more. The ratings translate directly into what you will be asked to review." },
    { h: "Why honest ratings matter", b: "Over-rating everything produces a paperwork mountain: every unit gets comprehensive documentation and gates, and your review time disappears before construction starts. Under-rating hides real risk: photo extraction rated Minimal gets no error-case analysis, and the failure surfaces in the demo.\n\nThe honest signal is the unit that scares you most — for this project it is usually photo extraction or chat+graphs. That one gets Comprehensive depth and the most of your review attention; the rest stay lean." },
    { h: "Common pitfalls", b: "Rating everything M is the lazy default and the most common mistake: the workflow then treats the vision pipeline and the income form as equals, generating the same documents for both. Rating income C buries a pure CRUD table in comprehensive paperwork you will never read. Under-rating photo extraction hides its error cases until the demo.\n\nRemember that ratings also drive question count: the workflow asks more, deeper questions for Comprehensive units at the gate. A C rating on chat+graphs means you will be asked about no-data states, tool-call failures, and chart edge cases — all decisions worth making now, while q4_4 already covers the extraction side. Rate honestly, and the ceremony lands where the risk is." },
    { h: "What a good answer includes", b: "Write the seven ratings in one line, exactly as the qbank example does. Add one sentence on your review-preference: do you want the workflow to ask fewer, deeper questions (Comprehensive style) or more, smaller ones (Standard style)? That is a preference, not a fact — say it and the agent adapts.\n\nName the unit you will review most carefully. The workflow uses your ratings to decide where to spend its own effort; telling it where you want depth is the point of the question." }
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Which unit scares you most to review — photo extraction or chat+graphs?", info: "That one gets Comprehensive depth and the most attention." },
    { q: "Do you want the workflow to ask fewer, deeper questions (Comprehensive) or more, smaller ones (Standard)?", info: "This is a preference, not a fact — say it and the agent adapts." }
  ],
  links: [
    "AGENTS.md — Requirements Analysis (repo)",
    "REFERENCES.md — AIDLC phases (repo)"
  ]
};

PAGES.q4_3 = {
  intro: "This page locks the extraction contract: the exact JSON the vision prompt must return from a receipt photo. The contract maps 1:1 to your locked schema, so defining it now becomes the prompt template later. It covers the fields, the accuracy reality, the prompt levers, and the validation rule that keeps the AI away from the database.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The contract fields", b: "The AI must return: store as text; date in ISO format; total as decimal plus currency; items as an optional array; category as a suggestion (the user overrides it); payment method mapped to the PaymentMethod list. SCHEMA.md has the entities: Expense, Category, Subcategory, Receipt, Currency, PaymentMethod — the contract is a projection of those tables.\n\nBecause the schema is locked, the contract is stable: whatever JSON you write today is the prompt template the agent will use in construction. A mismatch between contract and schema is the most expensive bug to fix later, because it touches the prompt, the validation layer, and the UI preview at once. Payment method values come from the PaymentMethod table (Cash, Card, Transfer, Nequi...), so the prompt should return the method name and let the API resolve it to the row. Subcategory is AI-generated on the fly per ALCANCE.md — the prompt suggests it, the user overrides it, and the schema keeps it as a required FK with a 'No subcategory' default." },
    { h: "Example JSON", b: "Drafting the example now is the single most useful thing this page can produce. A minimal valid response for a Colombian supermarket receipt:\n\n{\"store\": \"Exito\", \"date\": \"2026-08-09\", \"total\": 48500, \"currency\": \"COP\", \"payment_method\": \"Card\", \"category\": \"Food\", \"subcategory\": \"Groceries\", \"items\": [{\"name\": \"Rice 1kg\", \"amount\": 12500}, {\"name\": \"Eggs x30\", \"amount\": 9800}]}\n\nNote what is NOT in it: no invented fields, no exchange rate, no store address. If the AI cannot read the date, the field is null with a confidence note — never a guess. For a simple text entry ('spent $15 on lunch'), the same contract works with store = 'Manual' and items = [] — the schema explicitly supports this." },
    { h: "The accuracy reality", b: "Current GPT/Claude vision models reach roughly 90-94% extraction accuracy on clean receipts (2026 third-party OCR tests, see the link below). That means about 1 receipt in 10 comes back with an error — a wrong total, a misread store, a garbled date.\n\nThis is not a bug in the plan; it is why the confirm/edit/discard loop exists (ALCANCE.md, and q4_4). The contract must tolerate errors: it is a draft for the user to approve, not an automated pipeline into the database." },
    { h: "Prompt engineering levers", b: "Ask for strict JSON — the response must parse or the API rejects it. Forbid invented fields: 'if unsure, return null' beats a confident guess, because a null triggers user correction while a guess silently corrupts data. Request currency-symbol detection so '$12.50' and '12.500 COP' land in the right field.\n\nHandle thermal-paper text loss: faded receipts produce partial reads, so the prompt should prefer the printed total (the truth) over the sum of line items — SCHEMA.md notes the total may differ from the item sum due to taxes, discounts, and roundings." },
    { h: "The data contract rule", b: "The AI never writes to the database directly. It returns data to the API, which validates it against Pydantic/SQLModel models before saving. That single rule protects the DB from malformed AI output, duplicate writes, and injection-style surprises — and it is the pattern the AIDLC workflow expects.\n\nSchema details that matter for the contract: Receipt is required — every expense belongs to a receipt, even a manual one (store = 'Manual', image = null). Line items are optional drill-down detail; analytics run on the expense row (total × category), so the MVP contract can skip items without losing the dashboard." },
    { h: "What a good answer includes", b: "List the fields explicitly: store, date, total, items, category, payment method. Draft the exact example JSON for one receipt — that draft becomes the prompt template. Set the unreadable-field rule: null plus a confidence note, never a guess.\n\nDecide whether line items are required or optional for the MVP (recommended: optional). Your answer should read like a mini specification, because that is exactly what the agent will consume at the prompt-design step." }
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Draft the exact JSON the AI must return for one receipt.", info: "Writing the example JSON now becomes the prompt template later." },
    { q: "What does the AI do with a field it cannot read — null or guess?", info: "Rule: null + a 'confidence' note; guessing is how errors sneak in." },
    { q: "Line items: required or optional for the MVP?", info: "Optional — analytics run on the expense row; items are drill-down detail." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/SCHEMA.md — entities (repo)",
    "Claude vs GPT OCR comparison 2026 — https://codesota.com/ocr/claude-vs-gpt4o-ocr"
  ]
};

PAGES.q4_4 = {
  intro: "This page pre-answers the edge-case decisions of the pre-save flow: input → AI extracts → preview → Confirm / Edit / Discard. Blurry receipts, inaudible audio, and API failures are specified in ALCANCE.md, and your answers here define the error branches of DFD 1. It covers retry, fallback, discard semantics, and edit behavior.",
  sections: [
    { h: "The pre-save flow", b: "ALCANCE.md defines the flow: the user submits input (receipt photo, audio note, or text), the AI extracts structured data, the user sees a preview with editable fields, then Confirm saves to the DB, Edit changes fields and re-previews, Discard deletes everything and saves nothing.\n\nThis loop is the product's error net. It exists because extraction is not perfect (see q4_3: roughly 90-94% accuracy on clean receipts) and because inputs themselves fail — a blurry photo, an inaudible clip, an AI API outage. Every failure mode needs a defined UX branch, and those branches are what DFD 1 (TASKS.md Step 5 Stage 5) will draw." },
    { h: "Retry", b: "A retry button re-calls the AI with the same input. It is genuinely useful for transient API failures — a timeout, a 5xx, a rate limit — and genuinely useless for a photo that is unreadable: the second call sees the same blur.\n\nThe standard pattern is one automatic retry (with a short backoff) plus a manual button. Pair the button with a hint — 'better lighting, flat photo' — so the user improves the input instead of hammering retry. Decide this explicitly; it is one of the error branches DFD 1 must show." },
    { h: "Fallback message", b: "When extraction fails after retries, the user must never be stuck. The fallback is a friendly message — 'Couldn't read this receipt. Try a flatter photo or enter it manually.' — with a path into manual text entry.\n\nThe text-input fallback is the escape hatch: it converts a failure into a normal input method (feature 3 in FEATURES.md) instead of an error screen. Write the exact wording now; it will appear in the UI copy and in the DFD as the failure branch. The same fallback applies to audio: an inaudible clip gets the same message with a 'record again' hint. Log the failure reason server-side too — a one-line log entry is what you will look at when the demo hits a real API error." },
    { h: "Discard and edit semantics", b: "Discard is destructive and irreversible: it deletes everything and saves nothing. Two rules: always confirm with a dialog, even when the preview is empty, and delete the uploaded file too — not just the DB row — otherwise receipt images leak on disk over time (the same point as q3_2).\n\nEdit means inline field editing in the preview, then re-preview on save — the user fixes the AI's mistakes before anything touches the database. Decide whether editing re-runs extraction (it should not; the user's edits are the truth) and whether edits are validated against the same Pydantic/SQLModel rules as AI output (they should be)." },
    { h: "Where these branches live in the DFD", b: "DFD 1 (register expense, TASKS.md Step 5 Stage 5) draws the flow the professor will examine: input → AI call → success branch (preview → confirm/edit/discard) and failure branch (retry → fallback → manual entry). The Stage 7 validation checklist explicitly requires the confirmation loop to be present in the DFD, so these branches are not optional decoration.\n\nAudio deserves its own branch: an inaudible clip takes the same fallback path with a 'record again' hint instead of a photo hint. Text input rarely fails — it has no AI dependency when the user types the fields directly — so its error branch is just normal validation." },
    { h: "What a good answer includes", b: "Four concrete decisions: retry = one automatic retry plus a manual button with a lighting hint; fallback = the exact message text plus a manual-entry path; discard = always confirm and delete the file; edit = inline fields, re-preview, no re-extraction, same validation.\n\nState them as a list in your answer — these are the error branches DFD 1 will draw, so a list the agent can copy is worth more than prose. The qbank example is the model: keep Confirm/Edit/Discard plus retry; fallback message 'couldn't read this photo'; Discard deletes the file." }
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "On API failure: automatic retry (with backoff) or manual retry button?", info: "One automatic retry + a manual button is the standard pattern." },
    { q: "What exactly does the fallback message say?", info: "Write it now: 'Couldn't read this receipt. Try a flatter photo or enter it manually.'" },
    { q: "Discard: confirm dialog always, or only when there is data?", info: "Always confirm — it is destructive and irreversible." }
  ],
  links: [
    "docs/ALCANCE.md — Pre-save (repo)",
    "TASKS.md — Step 5 Stage 5, DFD 1 (repo)"
  ]
};

PAGES.q4_5 = {
  intro: "This page decides how your architecture diagrams are delivered: Mermaid text only, or Mermaid plus PNG exports via mmdc. The agent will ask this at the first diagram step, and the answer depends on how the professor reads the docs. It covers both options, the export mechanics, and the experimental C4 caveat.",
  sections: [
    { h: "Mermaid-only", b: "Mermaid diagrams are text in the repo — docs/ARCHITECTURE.md already holds the layer diagram as a Mermaid flowchart. GitHub and IDE viewers render them natively. Text diagrams are diff-friendly (a review comment can point at a line), and there is a single source of truth: the .md file.\n\nThe limitation is real: Mermaid source is invisible in Word or PDF exports of the docs. If the professor reads deliverables as exported documents, the diagrams simply disappear unless someone renders them. That is the entire trade-off. For a repo-first workflow like yours — all deliverables live in docs/ — this is usually an acceptable trade, because the docs are consumed on GitHub." },
    { h: "Mermaid + PNG via mmdc", b: "mmdc is mermaid-cli (@mermaid-js/mermaid-cli on npm). It needs Node plus a headless Chromium to render, and it exports diagrams as PNG, SVG, or PDF — visible in any document, not just on GitHub. mermaid.live is a lighter alternative with an export button if your machine struggles with Chromium.\n\nThe costs: every diagram edit requires a re-export or the PNG drifts from the source (drift risk), and the binary blobs bloat the repo. It is one npm install — acceptable on most machines — but it is a per-edit chore, not a one-time setup." },
    { h: "How the export workflow looks", b: "During development: edit the .md, view it on GitHub or in the IDE, commit text only. At a milestone: run mmdc once per diagram — mmdc -i arch.mmd -o arch.png (or .svg or .pdf, depending on where the document goes) — and commit the exports next to the .md. PNG is the universal choice; SVG scales without quality loss if the doc is digital.\n\nThe drift rule: the .md stays the source of truth; PNGs are artifacts and are never edited directly. Before the professor reviews, re-export everything so the artifacts match the source. If mmdc is a problem on your machine, mermaid.live's export button covers the same milestone need. Keep the exported files next to their source (for example docs/ARCHITECTURE.md plus docs/arch-layer.png), so a reader can find the artifact without hunting." },
    { h: "The C4 caveat", b: "Mermaid has C4 diagram syntax, but the official docs mark it experimental: the syntax and properties can change in future releases. TASKS.md Step 5 Stage 4 asks for a C4Component diagram for Auth, Expenses, Income, AI Agent/Tools, Chat, and File Storage.\n\nBecause C4 support is experimental, you may fall back to a labeled flowchart for the component diagram — your guide and the existing layer diagram already use flowcharts, and the validation checklist (labeled boxes, labeled arrows) applies to both. The flowchart fallback also keeps the component diagram consistent with the layer diagram style already reviewed in Stage 3. Decide now so the agent does not spend a session fighting experimental syntax." },
    { h: "The recommendation", b: "Mermaid-only during development, PNG export at milestones — specifically before the professor reviews. While the stack is TBD, Mermaid-only is the default: it costs nothing, keeps one source of truth, and defers the rendering question until a document actually needs to leave GitHub.\n\nThe milestone rule keeps the drift risk bounded: you export when there is something to show, not after every commit. If the professor later asks for a printed document, the milestone exports already exist and the conversion is a copy-paste away." },
    { h: "What a good answer includes", b: "State the choice and the reading format that drives it: GitHub → Mermaid-only is fine; Word/PDF → export PNGs at milestones. Confirm whether mmdc (Node + Chromium) is acceptable on your machine, or whether you will use mermaid.live's export button.\n\nNote the C4 decision: use the experimental C4Component syntax or fall back to a labeled flowchart (recommended while it is experimental). Your answer pre-fills the diagram deliverable rule for every stage of Step 5." }
  ],
  sub: [
    { q: "Will the professor read the docs in Word/PDF or on GitHub?", info: "GitHub → Mermaid-only is fine. Word/PDF → export PNGs at milestones." },
    { q: "Is mmdc (Node + Chromium) acceptable on your machine?", info: "It is one npm install; if your machine struggles, mermaid.live has an export button." }
  ],
  links: [
    "mermaid-cli (mmdc) — https://github.com/mermaid-js/mermaid-cli",
    "Mermaid C4 diagrams (experimental) — https://mermaid.ai/open-source/syntax/c4.html",
    "docs/guides/architecture-diagrams-guide.md — diagram stages (repo)"
  ]
};
